/**
 * The workflow contract, delivered in the initialize response (MCP
 * `instructions`). This is the single source of truth for how to use
 * nocetta — tool descriptions carry the mechanics; this carries the
 * when-and-why. Deliberately ~30 lines: it rides in every session's
 * context where the server connects, so it earns its place per line and
 * never restates what tools/list already says. Skills were rejected as
 * the delivery channel: loading them is model-mediated and unreliable,
 * while this arrives at handshake.
 */
export declare const INSTRUCTIONS = "Nocetta is anchored memory for this project: beliefs live as markdown files under .nocetta/ (committed to git) and detect their own staleness \u2014 a belief anchored to a code symbol or doc heading goes dirty when that code changes. Recall is current-only: dirty, superseded, and retired beliefs are never served as current.\n\nThe loop:\n- BEFORE editing a file, call memory_search with files_in_play \u2014 memories anchored to the files you are about to touch come back ranked and current-only. Use keyword for decision-shaped questions (\"why is retry capped at 3\").\n- AFTER finishing a task or refactor, call memory_worklist: dirty memories (the code each describes changed), cross-authority conflicts and duplicate pairs needing a supersede, and unreadable store files. Close every item \u2014 memory_repair with action \"reanchor\" when the belief is still true but the code moved, action \"retire\" (reason required) when the belief's subject is gone, memory_supersede to converge a duplicate pair (keep the better-anchored, better-worded belief as the tip).\n- CAPTURE with memory_remember at three moments: the user states a decision, preference, or constraint (\"always\u2026\", \"never\u2026\", \"we do X because Y\") \u2014 remember it immediately, and a user-stated directive is authority \"invariant\"; you make a non-obvious choice whose rationale will not survive in the diff \u2014 capture it before moving on; a task ends \u2014 before your summary, look back once at whether a future session needs anything from it. kind: \"claim\" = a fact about code behavior (anchor it: artifact_path + symbol_name, or heading for docs \u2014 unknown or ambiguous names are refused with the candidates; fix the name and retry), \"value\" = a decision and its why, \"lore-fact\" = a doc/plan fact, \"entity\" = a thing and its aliases. Give a one-line summary; shape the body as a lead sentence then **Why:**/**How to apply:**, with [[wikilinks]] to related nodes \u2014 a future recall previews the summary before spending body budget on the rest.\n\nStore what git cannot tell the next session: decisions, rationale, behavior facts, project vocabulary \u2014 the things a future session would otherwise re-derive or violate silently. Under-capture is the expensive error: a stale memory surfaces in the worklist to be repaired, but a lost decision is gone. Skip transient task state and what a diff already shows; the write gate refuses secrets. On contradiction, supersede (memory_supersede) \u2014 never write a conflicting duplicate; when remember returns a duplicate advisory the store already holds the belief, so supersede the named node instead of keeping both. History keeps superseded beliefs and memory_as_of can revisit them.\n\nHumans: `nocetta ls` browses the store, `nocetta ledger` shows which memories earn their keep; `nocetta check --strict` is the CI gate (exit 1 when memories need attention) \u2014 keep it green like tests.";
